> Can you explain what all of this does? I'm a bit lost and confused.

Absolutely. Think of this configuration as telling Angular:

> “When I run the frontend locally, serve Angular on port `4200`, but forward backend requests to Express on port `3000`.”

## The `serve` section

```json
"serve": {
  "builder": "@angular/build:dev-server",
  "options": {
    "proxyConfig": "src/proxy.conf.json"
  },
  "configurations": {
    "production": {
      "buildTarget": "frontend:build:production"
    },
    "development": {
      "buildTarget": "frontend:build:development"
    }
  },
  "defaultConfiguration": "development"
}
```

### `serve`

This controls what happens when you run:

```powershell
npm start
```

or:

```powershell
ng serve
```

It starts Angular’s local development server.

### `builder`

```json
"builder": "@angular/build:dev-server"
```

This tells Angular which program should run the development server.

You do not need to change this. It is the current builder generated by Angular.

### `options`

```json
"options": {
  "proxyConfig": "src/proxy.conf.json"
}
```

This contains general settings for the development server.

The important setting is:

```json
"proxyConfig": "src/proxy.conf.json"
```

It tells Angular to read proxy instructions from:

```text
frontend/src/proxy.conf.json
```

The path is relative to the `frontend` folder.

## The configurations

```json
"configurations": {
  "production": {
    "buildTarget": "frontend:build:production"
  },
  "development": {
    "buildTarget": "frontend:build:development"
  }
}
```

Angular has separate build modes.

The `production` configuration is used for optimized builds.

The `development` configuration is used while coding. It keeps source maps enabled and disables some optimizations to make debugging easier.

This line:

```json
"defaultConfiguration": "development"
```

means that running:

```powershell
ng serve
```

uses the development configuration automatically.

## The proxy file

Your `frontend/src/proxy.conf.json` should contain:

```json
{
  "/api/**": {
    "target": "http://127.0.0.1:3000",
    "secure": false
  },
  "/auth/**": {
    "target": "http://127.0.0.1:3000",
    "secure": false
  },
  "/games/**": {
    "target": "http://127.0.0.1:3000",
    "secure": false
  },
  "/streetview/**": {
    "target": "http://127.0.0.1:3000",
    "secure": false
  }
}
```

Each entry says:

> “If the browser requests this kind of URL from Angular, forward it to Express.”

For example, Angular code may call:

```typescript
fetch("/api/me")
```

The browser requests:

```text
http://localhost:4200/api/me
```

Angular sees `/api/**` and forwards the request to:

```text
http://127.0.0.1:3000/api/me
```

The response then comes back through Angular to the browser.

The request flow is:

```text
Browser
  |
  | http://localhost:4200/api/me
  v
Angular development server
  |
  | forwards to http://127.0.0.1:3000/api/me
  v
Express backend
```

This lets the Angular frontend use relative URLs such as:

```typescript
/api/me
/games/start
/streetview/access-token
```

without hard-coding the backend address everywhere.

## What the individual proxy settings mean

```json
"/api/**"
```

Matches API URLs such as:

```text
/api/me
/api/create-checkout-session
/api/checkout/confirm
```

The `**` means it also matches deeper paths.

```json
"target": "http://127.0.0.1:3000"
```

This is the address where your Express backend is running.

```json
"secure": false
```

This tells the proxy not to reject an HTTPS target with an untrusted certificate. Your local backend uses regular HTTP, so this setting does not cause a problem.

## Why use a proxy?

Without the proxy, Angular would need to call:

```typescript
fetch("http://localhost:3000/api/me")
```

That creates a different origin from the frontend at port `4200`, which can lead to CORS and cookie problems.

With the proxy, Angular calls:

```typescript
fetch("/api/me")
```

The browser considers the request same-origin because it is talking to port `4200`. Angular forwards it internally to Express.

This is especially important because your authentication uses an HTTP-only cookie.

## What happens in production?

This proxy is only for local development.

Later, when Angular is built for production, the proxy will not be included. The Angular files will be built and served through your existing deployment setup.

For now, run:

Terminal 1:

```powershell
npm run dev
```

Terminal 2, inside `frontend`:

```powershell
npm start
```

Then:

- Angular runs at `http://localhost:4200`
- Express runs at `http://localhost:3000`
- Angular forwards backend requests to Express

After changing `angular.json` or `proxy.conf.json`, restart `npm start`. Angular only reads the proxy configuration when the development server starts. [Angular proxy documentation](https://angular.dev/tools/cli/serve)
